= January 1923

French and Belgian troops have marched into the Ruhr. The Reich answers with passive resistance paid for by the printing press, and the mark is sliding towards worthlessness. Wages are eaten by prices within days, while the debts of heavy industry melt away. The Cuno government calls this national unity; for those who pay with their wages, it is class war conducted by other means.

We lead the KPD, a mass party that was burned by the March Action of 1921, split over Paul Levi, and told by Moscow to find the road to the workers who still follow the SPD. Our factions disagree about almost everything: whether to seek a united front with the Social Democratic leaders who drowned the revolution of 1918 and 1919 in blood, whether the crisis is the moment to strike, and how far to follow Moscow, where Lenin is gravely ill and his heirs are already fighting over the succession.

The workers will not wait for our debates to end, and neither will the nationalists in Bavaria. History does not promise that this crisis ends in revolution, or that we are ready for it. We have a year to find out.
